    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, what? I'm not sure I follow the goblin part. Can you just stick to the technical side? I'm trying to understand the actual physics. Also, you mentioned pressure differences—what happens if that tube gets blocked by something, like an insect or debris? How does that affect the reading?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's focus on the technical aspects.\n\nThe pitot tube works on the principle of Bernoulli's principle, which states that the pressure of a fluid (in this case, air) decreases as its velocity increases. When air flows into the pitot tube, it creates a region of lower pressure inside the tube compared to the surrounding air.\n\nNow, if the pitot tube gets blocked by something, like an insect or debris, it can cause a significant pressure difference between the inside and outside of the tube. This can lead to an inaccurate airspeed reading, as the pressure sensor may not be able to accurately measure the pressure difference.\n\nIn such cases, the airspeed reading may become stuck or oscillate erratically. To mitigate this issue, modern aircraft often have redundant pitot tubes or backup systems, such as pitot-static probes, which can provide a more accurate airspeed reading even if one of the primary pitot tubes is blocked.\n\nIt's worth noting that the pitot tube is designed to be relatively resistant to blockages, but it's not foolproof. In extreme cases, a blockage can cause the airspeed reading to become unreliable, which is why pilots need",
      "source": "target",
